ut who don't just swap to the other  gender  out of those two options because there  are far more than two options  there are non-binary people who fall  somewhere in the middle there are  agender people who just  kind of aren't on the spectrum at all  and there's gender fluid people  like me whose position on that spectrum  just doesn't really stay put  at all and  there is an easy way to connect the  doctor  in general to a trans experience  by way of regeneration that's  not how i'm gonna connect though for a  number of reasons firstly as a gender  fluid person my  trans experience doesn't match the  template that  a lot of people think of in terms of  well i was this but i realized i'm this  and so now i'm going to live like that  my own journey has been  very gradual it has been a slow  evolution slow discovery and a slow  change  of my understanding of who i am i've  talked about this on my other channel  that's probably a good primer video to  get a general sense  of how i approach myself  and my own evolution but  that arc that slow  gradual change for me  and from my understanding of myself is a  big part of why i latch on to capaldi's  doctor  because i would argue that the 12th  doctor  is really the only one with a truly  clear character arc  most of the other doctors change a  little  over the course of their time they may  soften a little if they started out a  bit rough  first doctor kind of did that and if you  listen to big finish  the sixth doctor did as well they can  open up and they can become more  personable as opposed to more closed off  eccleston's ninth doctor did that but  that's  not quite the same as an arc it's a bit  of an arc because it's a  change it's them becoming more  comfortable with who they're around and  opening up to them but that's really  just revealing more of who they are  the fundamental cores of who they are  aren't really changing  they're just revealing more of it to the  people around them  capaldi's doctor goes through clear  changes in terms of his understanding of  who he is and how it is  best for him to exemplify that  over those three series i've seen people  criticize his time as being inconsistent  because the way that he behaves in  series eight  is not the same way he behaves in series  nine it's not the same way he behaves in  series ten  now if you don't view it as an  arc yes that can seem inconsistent and  it can be a little bit jarring that it  kind of  seems to jump in between series but for  me it makes 100  sense series 8  he spends the entire time asking  questions like  am i a good man he doesn't know who he  is  he knows something isn't quite clicking  he knows some basics he knows some  fundamentals he knows what he  wants to fight for but as a  person he isn't really sure  of who he is or who he's supposed to be  and by the end he's found something to  latch on to  with that speech that he gives to missy  in death in heaven i am  an idiot passing through helping out  learning and then in series nine he's  embraced that  very fully he has really leaned into the  idea that he's just  he's just an idiot passing through and  trying to help possibly leaned into it  a bit too hard with the sunglasses with  the guitar  with the more casual look with the  hoodies and you could think of that  as god they really just wrote him like  garbage like no  as someone who's had to go through  periods of self-discovery  one of the very common things when  you reach a point where you go okay i  think this is where i'm at  is to dive into it maybe a little harder  than you needed to  sometimes you need to over correct  and then pedal back and actually find  the middle ground where you're supposed  to be  and to me that's what his journey from  series 8  grumpy to series 9 flamboyant  to series 10 where it feels like he has  found  the balance of everything where it feels  like he still  can be fun and silly  yet has not lost that sense of gravity  and he does not let that sense of fun  cause a dangerous situation  that speaks to me a lot  because i can look back to my earlier  days  before i really thought to identify  myself as gender fluid  i would identify as a cross dresser for  a very long time  and back then when i would get into a  feminine mode i would have to go all out  i would have to body shave wig full  makeup  short skirts high heels i needed  all of that to feel like i was  connecting with my femininity  i was overdoing it i found something  that like yes this is definitely a part  of who i am  but i couldn't just explore i dove  headlong into it like the doctor  dove headlong into being the idiot with  a box and a screwdriver in series nine  and then over time as i got more  comfortable  with myself i was able to feel  myself even in a feminine mindset  with a few small touches not  needing every expectation there was a  point when i did need it  but then over time no i don't need all  this  i can find a better balance for myself  and here's the big thing that journey  for me  took time and this doctor's journey  takes time  that means so much to me because i feel  like so often  character arcs for shows like this  can feel rushed or minimal like i said  the way  that i can perceive an arc in most of  the other doctors especially in the  modern era  is basically them either opening up a  little bit more about themselves or  finding the ability to reconnect with  people again after something rough  happened that kind of applies to smith  for instance but that to me  is really only an arc in terms of how  they relate to other people  it's not an arc about how they think  about themselves  and the one other time that i would say  that kind of happens is with tenant and  with things like waters of mars and time  lord victorious which i know has now  become a whole spin-off thing which i  haven't gotten into so  maybe it's better explored there but  sticking to the show yeah no that kind  of just happens quickly and then it's  done  it's rushed but to watch  capaldi over time  question who he is grapple with what  that means  overdo it and then  dial it back and find the happy medium  and to be so assured of who he was  that he was scared to change again  at the end of the doctor falls leading  into  twice upon a time he says  i don't want to change again  never again i can't keep on being  somebody else  and my reading of that part of it is he  has fought  so hard and so long and gone through so  much  to become who he is now the idea that he  might lose that  in an instant is  far more terrifying than the idea that  he might have just  died if he died he would have died as  the person he had fought  and worked and suffered  in order to become and because he  doesn't know what will happen with  regeneration  all of that could change and maybe when  he regenerates  he'll be comfortable in whatever new  identity and sense of self that he has  or maybe maybe the doctor will end up  right back at square one and have to go  through this whole journey again  after everything that he went through  and that is terrifying  the idea of your journey of  self-exploration  of your understanding of yourself  getting undone  is terrifying and i feel like he  captures that fear  and i feel like he earned it by allowing  the audience in to watch him understand  with key moments like the end  of death in heaven like the girl  who died when he realizes why he has  this face  and it is to help him stay the course  and become  the best most true version of himself  that he can  to that declaration and the doctor falls  about  who he is and his refusal to be anything  else  other than that you know maybe there's  no point in any of this  at all but it's the best i can do so i'm  gonna do it and i will stand here  doing it till it kills me you're going  to die too that  journey watching him  not just change from his experience but  change because he is  questioning and looking at and carefully  examining  himself and trying to better know  himself  not for the sake of other people but  because he needs to sort his own  life  that speaks to me so deeply  and it's why i said at the start  i won't rule out the possibility that  another doctor might become my favorite  at some point  but i will be very surprised  i have loved so many incarnations of the  doctor  classic and modern  but i don't have any other  that speaks to my life  my journey it feels like it captures  my struggles and my  fears the way  that peter capaldi's 12th doctor does  and for me  the arc of his character  probably unintentionally but  serves as a beautiful  allegory for lgbtq plus self-discovery  at least in regards to how my journey  went i don't know how many folks have  much interest in this take  but i needed to share it because it's so  core  to what i love about him  what is it about your favorite doctor  that helps you  really connect with them whatever it is  or whatever your thoughts are on  anything i've just said politely leave  your comments down below  and let's talk about it i have a patreon  that is what keeps this entire thing up  and running and there are rewards  for every tier of support there's also  youtube memberships now  with some different rewards if you'd  rather go that way but if you're not  able to  like share subscribe the usual youtube  stuff helps me out as well but  don't even feel too much pressure about  that the thing that i really want you to  remember  is that you are beautiful you are valid  you are loved you are the council  and i only run the meetings and until  next time  this council is adjourned  [Music]  [Music]  um  [Music]  um  [Music]

